cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this, please cite it as below."
title: "Width vs depth for fact storage in small language models (E02-E05)"
authors:
  - family-names: Killaly
    given-names: Johnathon
date-released: 2026-11-09
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/johnathonkillaly/width-vs-depth-facts"
abstract: >-
  Four preregistered pilots on ~21M-parameter proxy LMs (13.2M non-embedding, 40M tokens, 1,920 synthetic facts).
  At equal non-embedding parameters a wide-shallow shape stores more facts than a narrow-deep one; with an equal
  tied word table about half of the lead remains. With facts seen up to 1,024 times (E05), every shape stores
  them all, and the deep shape needs about twice the repetitions to reach the same recall.
